.TH HYPRFETCH 1 "2026-11-19" "hyprfetch 0.3.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hyprfetch \- Hyprland-native fetch utility for system, session and rice state
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hyprfetch
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hyprfetch
prints the state of the machine and of the running Hyprland session next to a
logo, in the style of a classic fetch screenshot. It reports the
operating system, the hardware, the desktop theme, and the live compositor
state such as the active workspace, the focused window, the monitors, the
layer-shell surfaces and the running desktop daemons.
.PP
Modules that need Hyprland report why they are unavailable instead of
aborting the run, so the tool stays usable on a plain desktop.
.PP
By default the logo is the running distribution's own mark, chosen from
.I os-release
and drawn in solid pixel blocks; when the distribution is not known, the
generic Linux logo is used. The
.B \-\-logo
option selects a different preset, such as the optional
.B hypr-pixel
wordmark.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Read the configuration from
.I PATH
instead of the default location.
.TP
.B \-\-init\-config
Write a starter configuration to the default location. An existing file is
never overwritten.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-config
Print the default configuration to standard output and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Ignore the configuration file for this run.
.TP
.B \-\-retro
Force the arcade styling on. It is already the default; the flag exists so a
config with
.B retro = false
can be overridden for one run.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-retro
Disable the default arcade styling and print the plain fetch look.
.TP
.B \-\-fast
Skip the expensive probes (CPU sampling, GPU vendor tools, audio, network and
media commands) for a near-instant render. Every row stays, with the values
that need a probe omitted rather than faked.
.TP
.BI \-\-logo\-image " path"
Draw the PNG at
.I path
as the logo, using the kitty graphics protocol (kitty, ghostty and WezTerm).
The information rows are placed to the right of the picture and the arcade
view is turned off for that run. Where the protocol is unavailable, or the file
is missing, is not a PNG, or exceeds 8 MB, the text logo is used instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-logo\-image\-cols " n"
The widest the image may be drawn, in terminal cells. The default of 0 derives
the width from the number of information rows, so a square picture fills the
height of the text block, capped at half the terminal. A non-zero value is used
as the ceiling and the picture keeps its aspect ratio inside it.
.TP
.B \-\-icons
Force the Nerd Font glyph prefix on. This is already the default; the flag
exists so a config with
.B icons = false
can be overridden for one run.
.TP
.B \-\-redact
Replace the values that identify the machine before printing: the account name,
hostname, private IPv4 and MAC addresses, the Wi-Fi SSID and hardware serials.
Rows, labels and columns are untouched, so the output still shows the layout.
Use it before a screenshot goes into a README, an issue or a release page.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-icons
Print the plain
.B [LABEL]
tags without a glyph. Use this on a terminal whose font lacks the Nerd Font
private-use glyphs, where they would otherwise render as empty boxes.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Print plain key and value rows without the logo, the header or the colour
bars.
.TP
.B \-\-share
Use the share preset, which leads with the theme and the live session
state. It is meant for rice screenshots.
.TP
.BI \-\-animation " " \fISTYLE\fR
How the output is revealed on a terminal:
.B split
grows the logo skeleton first and then lights the session rows up behind it,
.B wave
lights the logo cells column by column from the left edge,
.B lines
reveals one row after another, and
.B static
turns every motion off, spinner included. Defaults to
.BR split ,
and the finished frame is the same static output whatever the style.
.TP
.B \-\-watch
Keep running and redraw the output in place. The screen is only cleared
when standard output is a terminal.
.TP
.BI \-\-watch\-interval " " \fISECONDS\fR
How long to wait between two refreshes of
.BR \-\-watch .
Defaults to 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-style " " \fISTYLE\fR
The layout to use:
.B split
places the logo next to the rows,
.B compact
drops the logo and prints one row per module, and
.B minimal
prints a bare
.IR key : value
list.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " " \fIMODE\fR
When to colour the output:
.B auto
follows the terminal and the
.B NO_COLOR
environment variable,
.B always
colours it and
.B never
turns it off.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-modules " " \fIMODULES\fR
A comma separated list of the modules to show. The options configured for
these modules in the configuration file are kept.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " " \fIPALETTE\fR
The colour palette. Built in are
.BR hypr ,
.BR mono ,
.BR catppuccin ,
.BR dracula ,
.BR gruvbox ,
.BR gruvbox\-light ,
.BR nord ,
.BR one\-dark ,
.BR solarized\-dark ,
.BR solarized\-light ,
.BR rose\-pine ,
.BR ayu ,
.BR everforest ,
.BR kanagawa ,
.BR monokai ,
.BR material
and
.BR github .
Run
.B hyprfetch \-\-list\-themes
for the authoritative names.
.TP
.BI \-\-logo " " \fIPRESET\fR
The logo to use. Besides the distribution logos there are
.B minimal
for the wordmark and
.B none
to hide the logo. Run
.B hyprfetch \-\-list\-logos
for the full list.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the collected modules as JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-diagnostics
Print the status of every module, including the ones that are
unavailable, and a summary of the counts.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-logos
Print the names of the available logos and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-modules
Print the names of the available modules and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-themes
Print the names of the available palettes and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short help text and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file is read from
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hyprfetch/config.toml
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, and from
.I ~/.config/hyprfetch/config.toml
otherwise.
.PP
It holds the appearance, the logo, the order of the modules, the options of
each module and the command plugins. The order of the
.B [[modules]]
entries is the order they are printed in, and an entry may carry its own
options:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[[modules]]
name = "disk"
mounts = ["/", "/home"]

[[modules]]
name = "hypr-devices"
compact = false
.RE
.fi
.PP
The
.B mounts
option of the disk module lists the mount points it reports. The
.B compact
option of a module switches it between a single summary row and one row per
detail. The
.B list
option of the binds, layers, rules, shortcuts and layouts modules adds one row
per entry, and the
.B devices
option of the devices module does the same for the input devices.
.SH PLUGINS
A plugin runs a command and turns its output into rows, which lets a user
add information that
.B hyprfetch
does not know about:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[[plugins]]
name = "music"
command = "sh"
args = ["-c", "printf 'Track\\t%s\\n' \\"Lo-fi radio\\""]
timeout_ms = 1000
.RE
.fi
.PP
A single plain line becomes one row using the
.B label
or the
.B name
of the plugin.
.B Key<TAB>Value
and
.B Key=Value
lines become explicit rows. A plugin that fails or runs into its timeout
keeps the reason in the output.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
The directory the configuration is read from.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Keeps the colour out of the output when
.B \-\-color auto
is used.
.TP
.B USER ", " USERNAME ", " HOSTNAME
Used for the session title.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hyprfetch
exits with 0 when it printed its output, and with a non zero status when it
could not run at all. A module that is unavailable does not change the
exit status.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the default output:
.PP
.nf
.RS
hyprfetch
.RE
.fi
.PP
A screenshot for a rice:
.PP
.nf
.RS
hyprfetch --share
.RE
.fi
.PP
Only a few modules, without the logo:
.PP
.nf
.RS
hyprfetch --plain --modules os,kernel,memory,hyprland
.RE
.fi
.PP
Keep the session state on screen:
.PP
.nf
.RS
hyprfetch --watch --watch-interval 2
.RE
.fi
.PP
Draw the logo as a wave sweeping from the left edge:
.PP
.nf
.RS
hyprfetch --animation wave
.RE
.fi
.PP
Check what a machine without Hyprland would report:
.PP
.nf
.RS
hyprfetch --diagnostics
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hyprctl (1)
.PP
The wiki at
.UR https://github.com/polarours/hyprfetch
.UE
